WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.577 --> 00:00:03.364
- This week on 'Couch Tomatoes,'
we're talking HBO's 'Watchmen'

00:00:03.364 --> 00:00:05.535
and giving you our Fresh
Picks on what to watch.

00:00:05.535 --> 00:00:07.609
What's up guys I'm Naz Perez,

00:00:07.609 --> 00:00:09.069
welcome back to 'Couch Tomatoes,'

00:00:09.069 --> 00:00:10.424
sponsored by Freschetta Pizza.

00:00:10.424 --> 00:00:12.260
This is a show where we
talk about the latest TV

00:00:12.260 --> 00:00:13.648
and why you should tune in.

00:00:13.648 --> 00:00:15.171
And today on the show we are diving deep

00:00:15.171 --> 00:00:16.650
into HBO's 'Watchmen.'

00:00:16.650 --> 00:00:19.072
I am so stoked 'cause
joining me on the coach today

00:00:19.072 --> 00:00:21.528
is Shane Madej and Ryan Bergara
from Buzzfeed's 'Unsolved.'

00:00:21.528 --> 00:00:23.062
Welcome back, welcome back.

00:00:23.062 --> 00:00:23.987
- Happy to be here. (laughs)

00:00:23.987 --> 00:00:26.686
- Again, happy to be back on the couch.

00:00:26.686 --> 00:00:27.848
- I'm excited you guys are here

00:00:27.848 --> 00:00:30.153
and the beautiful Roxy
Striar from Collider.

00:00:30.153 --> 00:00:32.594
- Hi guys, happy to be
here from the first time.

00:00:32.594 --> 00:00:34.145
- And you guys have seen Watchmen yeah?

00:00:34.145 --> 00:00:34.978
- Yes we have.

00:00:34.978 --> 00:00:35.811
- Yes.

00:00:35.811 --> 00:00:36.644
- Oh for sure.

00:00:36.644 --> 00:00:37.477
- Oh, for sure.

00:00:37.477 --> 00:00:39.598
I joke because they are
major fans so I'm so stoked

00:00:39.598 --> 00:00:41.398
to get into this with you guys.

00:00:41.398 --> 00:00:42.231
So let's do this.

00:00:42.231 --> 00:00:44.663
Set in present day three
decades after the release

00:00:44.663 --> 00:00:46.388
after the graphic novel by Alan Moore,

00:00:46.388 --> 00:00:48.173
which takes place in the 1980s.

00:00:48.173 --> 00:00:50.696
The new series is already
getting a lot of praise

00:00:50.696 --> 00:00:53.200
from critics and is
currently a Certified Fresh

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:55.500
with a 98% on the Tomatometer,

00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:57.610
at the time of this recording.

00:00:57.610 --> 00:01:00.244
Now guys Damon Lindelof, the
creator, has been very open

00:01:00.244 --> 00:01:02.534
about the fact that this is not a remake

00:01:02.534 --> 00:01:05.802
but a remixed continuation
of the 1986 comic.

00:01:05.802 --> 00:01:07.690
But I know 'Watchmen' is very polarizing.

00:01:07.690 --> 00:01:10.044
So I need to know, what
do people need to know

00:01:10.044 --> 00:01:11.125
when they tune into this show?

00:01:11.125 --> 00:01:12.612
Do they need to read the graphic novel

00:01:12.612 --> 00:01:15.585
or have seen Zack Snyder's
film that came out in 2009

00:01:15.585 --> 00:01:16.839
to know what's going on?

00:01:16.839 --> 00:01:19.893
- Well this is much more
relevant to the novel,

00:01:19.893 --> 00:01:22.110
as opposed to the movie.

00:01:22.110 --> 00:01:24.903
We see things in episode
one, like the squid falling

00:01:24.903 --> 00:01:27.361
from the sky that is
reminiscent of the novel

00:01:27.361 --> 00:01:29.358
and the ending there, not the movie.

00:01:29.358 --> 00:01:31.465
But I think that you
could go into this blind

00:01:31.465 --> 00:01:32.779
if you really wanted to.

00:01:32.779 --> 00:01:35.642
They are telling their own
story with new characters

00:01:35.642 --> 00:01:37.891
and so it kind of depends
on how deep of a dive

00:01:37.891 --> 00:01:39.296
you want to do beforehand.

00:01:39.296 --> 00:01:40.910
But they're really trying to make it,

00:01:40.910 --> 00:01:43.670
so that if you are a
new fan to 'Watchmen,'

00:01:43.670 --> 00:01:46.968
that you can be a new fan
and still enjoy the series.

00:01:46.968 --> 00:01:48.076
- Do you guys think the same thing?

00:01:48.076 --> 00:01:49.680
Because I thought that at
first but then I was like

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:51.991
I don't know they're things
like 'The Watchmen's Son'

00:01:51.991 --> 00:01:52.931
is the name of the play

00:01:52.931 --> 00:01:54.634
that Jeremy Irons' character puts on.

00:01:54.634 --> 00:01:55.797
I'm like, how would you know?

00:01:55.797 --> 00:01:56.634
What do you guys think?

00:01:56.634 --> 00:01:57.944
- I'm trying to put myself in the brain

00:01:57.944 --> 00:02:00.383
of someone whose not familiar with it.

00:02:00.383 --> 00:02:02.142
I feel like I would be overwhelmed.

00:02:02.142 --> 00:02:04.262
I feel like people should
look at their calendar,

00:02:04.262 --> 00:02:06.880
figure out how much free time they have.

00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:09.667
If you're a busy person, watch the movie.

00:02:09.667 --> 00:02:10.545
You'll sort of get it

00:02:10.545 --> 00:02:13.885
without the very good ending of the squid.

00:02:13.885 --> 00:02:16.933
If you have more time, read the novel.

00:02:16.933 --> 00:02:19.421
It's so good and it's very readable.

00:02:19.421 --> 00:02:20.560
It's not daunting.

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:23.052
- I will say in today's
age, though, to make someone

00:02:23.052 --> 00:02:24.592
have a homework assignment
before they have

00:02:24.592 --> 00:02:27.646
to enjoy this piece of work is a lot.

00:02:27.646 --> 00:02:29.464
I actually think you can go into it blind.

00:02:29.464 --> 00:02:31.359
I think it would help
obviously to have some

00:02:31.359 --> 00:02:32.482
of the prior materials

00:02:32.482 --> 00:02:35.053
but I do think it's very
watchable on its own.

00:02:35.053 --> 00:02:37.214
- Right and I love that, watchable.

00:02:37.214 --> 00:02:40.908
And if you guys go to HBO.com/Watchmen,

00:02:40.908 --> 00:02:43.325
they created this Pdpedia
and they have a lot

00:02:43.325 --> 00:02:46.033
of source material there
to go along with the show.

00:02:46.033 --> 00:02:47.977
So I think that's kind of
an easy way to get into it.

00:02:47.977 --> 00:02:48.810
- [Shane] I didn't even know it.

00:02:48.810 --> 00:02:50.601
- But yeah, I want to get
into the controversies

00:02:50.601 --> 00:02:51.706
of making the show.

00:02:51.706 --> 00:02:53.697
But before I do I'm gonna
kick it back to a convo

00:02:53.697 --> 00:02:55.859
I had with Roxy about the
best way to prep before

00:02:55.859 --> 00:02:58.258
a big TV binge, check it out.

00:02:58.258 --> 00:03:00.458
Roxy are you so excited
to be on the show today?

00:03:00.458 --> 00:03:02.262
- Yes thank you so much for having me over

00:03:02.262 --> 00:03:05.048
to watch some episodes
and just kind of relax.

00:03:05.048 --> 00:03:06.142
- Relax?

00:03:06.142 --> 00:03:07.750
You can't relax, we have to stay active

00:03:07.750 --> 00:03:09.002
while we're watching TV.

00:03:09.002 --> 00:03:10.583
- What do you mean active?

00:03:10.583 --> 00:03:13.166
- You know active like
standing up to go get food,

00:03:13.166 --> 00:03:15.698
walking all the way over to the kitchen

00:03:15.698 --> 00:03:18.135
stretching to get that bomb food photo.

00:03:18.135 --> 00:03:19.544
It's all exercise.

00:03:19.544 --> 00:03:21.307
- Okay, that's the kind
of exercise I can actually

00:03:21.307 --> 00:03:22.180
get behind.

00:03:22.180 --> 00:03:24.057
- Okay good, because after our workouts,

00:03:24.057 --> 00:03:25.280
I have a reward for us.

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:27.958
I made us this unreal
Freschetta brick oven

00:03:27.958 --> 00:03:29.286
spinach and mushroom pizza.

00:03:29.286 --> 00:03:31.586
The crust is so crunchy and amazing.

00:03:31.586 --> 00:03:33.566
And they're all these
mushrooms and spinach on it,

00:03:33.566 --> 00:03:34.858
which are basically superfoods.

00:03:34.858 --> 00:03:36.970
- 100% and thank goodness for pizza

00:03:36.970 --> 00:03:39.268
because who knew watching
TV was so much work?

00:03:39.268 --> 00:03:40.629
- Listen it is a tough job

00:03:40.629 --> 00:03:41.626
but someone's got to do it.

00:03:41.626 --> 00:03:43.011
(both laugh)

00:03:43.011 --> 00:03:46.251
All right guys so when the
'Watchmen' graphic novel came out

00:03:46.251 --> 00:03:48.445
it was groundbreaking in many ways.

00:03:48.445 --> 00:03:50.142
Mainly because it was reflection

00:03:50.142 --> 00:03:51.340
of our deepest fears, right.

00:03:51.340 --> 00:03:54.125
Like the Cold War was top
of mind, nuclear apocalypse

00:03:54.125 --> 00:03:56.400
was still a threat and I feel
like this 'Watchmen' series

00:03:56.400 --> 00:03:59.468
deals with some heavy issues
as well, mainly racism.

00:03:59.468 --> 00:04:02.512
But in the same bold fashion
way that comic book does.

00:04:02.512 --> 00:04:05.293
So why do you think Watchmen,
the series is so effective

00:04:05.293 --> 00:04:07.376
at tackling these issues?

00:04:08.471 --> 00:04:11.398
- I'll say that something about the sort

00:04:11.398 --> 00:04:13.931
of alternative reality that they craft.

00:04:13.931 --> 00:04:17.846
It's why science-fiction
works because you've created

00:04:17.846 --> 00:04:20.865
this world that is not
totally recognizable.

00:04:20.865 --> 00:04:22.160
There's something off in it.

00:04:22.160 --> 00:04:24.727
So the stuff that's true or that reflects

00:04:24.727 --> 00:04:28.111
our own society really
kind of, it really pops.

00:04:28.111 --> 00:04:31.944
So I've heard people say
that it seems strange

00:04:33.061 --> 00:04:35.734
that they're going with
the Tulsa Race Riots

00:04:35.734 --> 00:04:36.567
and all this other stuff.

00:04:36.567 --> 00:04:40.817
But the other series, the
comic did tackle Vietnam,

00:04:41.831 --> 00:04:44.662
so they're handling real life issues.

00:04:44.662 --> 00:04:46.595
- And I will preface
whatever I'm about to say

00:04:46.595 --> 00:04:48.501
with I am a chucklehead.

00:04:48.501 --> 00:04:51.543
So there's not a weight (laughs)
to what I'm about to say,

00:04:51.543 --> 00:04:53.023
so take that with a grain of salt.

00:04:53.023 --> 00:04:55.463
However, I will say I think
there's something about Watchmen

00:04:55.463 --> 00:04:58.686
being a series where the
characters are looking inward

00:04:58.686 --> 00:05:00.301
at their own morality,

00:05:00.301 --> 00:05:02.399
which is, it's like this
introspective experience

00:05:02.399 --> 00:05:05.475
which when it's against the
backdrop of all these fears

00:05:05.475 --> 00:05:09.301
of the time, it kind of results
in people looking inward

00:05:09.301 --> 00:05:11.440
at themselves when
they're viewing perhaps.

00:05:11.440 --> 00:05:12.505
I don't know if that makes sense.

00:05:12.505 --> 00:05:13.390
- No, it does make sense.

00:05:13.390 --> 00:05:16.819
I think people, I mean the series opens

00:05:16.819 --> 00:05:19.597
with a depiction of the
Tulsa Race Massacre right.

00:05:19.597 --> 00:05:22.178
That's very powerful and when
it's told through Watchmen

00:05:22.178 --> 00:05:23.867
or any sort of superhero story,

00:05:23.867 --> 00:05:25.765
I think it gets people to pay attention.

00:05:25.765 --> 00:05:27.168
I think that's powerful.

00:05:27.168 --> 00:05:29.414
- I think that 'Watchmen''s
never been afraid

00:05:29.414 --> 00:05:30.604
to take a big swing.

00:05:30.604 --> 00:05:32.398
And that might work for some people,

00:05:32.398 --> 00:05:33.709
that might not work for others.

00:05:33.709 --> 00:05:35.665
But they are swinging right now

00:05:35.665 --> 00:05:37.749
and they are saying,
we're not afraid to tackle

00:05:37.749 --> 00:05:39.581
these massive issues in the same way

00:05:39.581 --> 00:05:41.773
that the original graphic
novel was not afraid.

00:05:41.773 --> 00:05:44.462
So it's a different issue
now that we are trying

00:05:44.462 --> 00:05:46.386
to talk about but it's the same concept

00:05:46.386 --> 00:05:49.449
that they are saying, okay,
we have a stance on this

00:05:49.449 --> 00:05:50.592
and we're going to take that stance.

00:05:50.592 --> 00:05:52.058
- Right, and I love the
stance that they're taking

00:05:52.058 --> 00:05:54.527
because we never saw diverse superheros

00:05:54.527 --> 00:05:56.606
in the Watchmen universe until now.

00:05:56.606 --> 00:05:58.847
And now the fact that Regina
King is the face of it.

00:05:58.847 --> 00:06:00.343
You know I think that's pretty incredible.

00:06:00.343 --> 00:06:02.822
But I want to ask you guys
because I know you're superfans

00:06:02.822 --> 00:06:04.939
and 'Watchmen' is so beloved 'cause

00:06:04.939 --> 00:06:06.246
there could be this argument here right

00:06:06.246 --> 00:06:08.795
that this could have
been a standalone series.

00:06:08.795 --> 00:06:10.556
Alan Moore, the creator,
has been very open.

00:06:10.556 --> 00:06:12.564
He doesn't like people
touching 'Watchmen.'

00:06:12.564 --> 00:06:13.882
He doesn't like people adapting it.

00:06:13.882 --> 00:06:16.629
He's not in the credits
of the show or the movie.

00:06:16.629 --> 00:06:17.905
Do you agree with Moore?

00:06:17.905 --> 00:06:20.202
Do you feel like 'Watchmen'
should be untouched?

00:06:20.202 --> 00:06:22.129
Or do you like this continued adaptation?

00:06:22.129 --> 00:06:26.489
- In my head his voice kind
of sounds like ra, ra, ra.

00:06:26.489 --> 00:06:27.322
(everybody laughs)

00:06:27.322 --> 00:06:28.155
- [Naz] He's like Scrooge.

00:06:28.155 --> 00:06:28.988
- Kind of.

00:06:28.988 --> 00:06:29.821
- [Shane] He's gonna come after you.

00:06:29.821 --> 00:06:30.948
- I know.

00:06:30.948 --> 00:06:33.371
- You guys would know the
next Buzzfeed's 'Unsolved.'

00:06:33.371 --> 00:06:36.779
- I love him and I love Dave
Gibbons for what they created

00:06:36.779 --> 00:06:38.962
because it was something that
was really impactful for me.

00:06:38.962 --> 00:06:40.569
And it is his baby.

00:06:40.569 --> 00:06:42.957
So I get why he wouldn't
want it to be touched

00:06:42.957 --> 00:06:44.440
when he's not a part of it.

00:06:44.440 --> 00:06:45.402
I really do get it.

00:06:45.402 --> 00:06:48.299
I'm a creator and that's really
challenging for somebody.

00:06:48.299 --> 00:06:51.714
That being said, if you
can't work on the art,

00:06:51.714 --> 00:06:53.862
I still want the art to be worked on.

00:06:53.862 --> 00:06:55.562
Because I still want to see more,

00:06:55.562 --> 00:06:58.278
as somebody, pun intended,
I want to see "Moore."

00:06:58.278 --> 00:06:59.111
Just not Alan Moore.

00:06:59.111 --> 00:06:59.944
(Shane laughs)

00:06:59.944 --> 00:07:01.203
As somebody who loves 'Watchmen,'

00:07:01.203 --> 00:07:03.760
how does that grow today in 2019?

00:07:03.760 --> 00:07:06.111
How do we move that story forward?

00:07:06.111 --> 00:07:07.841
And just because he can't work on it,

00:07:07.841 --> 00:07:09.955
doesn't mean there aren't
brilliant minds out there

00:07:09.955 --> 00:07:12.291
like Damon Lindelof who can tackle it.

00:07:12.291 --> 00:07:15.039
- Yeah, I was going to say
Lindelof of all people...

00:07:15.039 --> 00:07:17.644
I know 'Watchmen,' even
before the movie came out.

00:07:17.644 --> 00:07:21.467
There were versions of the
script that are horrible.

00:07:21.467 --> 00:07:23.233
(laughs) They're not good.

00:07:23.233 --> 00:07:25.504
And they eventually turned it into a movie

00:07:25.504 --> 00:07:27.733
that a lot of people like, some
people have issues with it.

00:07:27.733 --> 00:07:32.685
But having someone like Lindelof
who is a pretty thoughtful,

00:07:32.685 --> 00:07:36.915
creative person, he's made a lot of shows

00:07:36.915 --> 00:07:39.821
that really challenge people
and have a lot of new ideas.

00:07:39.821 --> 00:07:44.052
So it's sort of an ideal
situation as far as.

00:07:44.052 --> 00:07:45.740
- He's also pretty good
at handling universes

00:07:45.740 --> 00:07:47.036
and world-building.

00:07:47.036 --> 00:07:48.794
I think it's also just
like a curious question

00:07:48.794 --> 00:07:49.627
to think about.

00:07:49.627 --> 00:07:52.790
Like is the creator always the
best person to move a story

00:07:52.790 --> 00:07:53.775
forward like you were saying?

00:07:53.775 --> 00:07:56.035
Like we say that with
Lucas and 'Star Wars'

00:07:56.035 --> 00:07:58.275
and even Stephen King having his issues

00:07:58.275 --> 00:08:00.775
with the original 'Shining.' (laughs)

00:08:00.775 --> 00:08:02.525
But it is a question,

00:08:03.657 --> 00:08:05.138
I don't know if I have the answer to it.

00:08:05.138 --> 00:08:08.819
I do think it's, I wish he
would be onboard (laughs)

00:08:08.819 --> 00:08:10.374
as I guess what I will say to that.

00:08:10.374 --> 00:08:12.813
- Yeah, but I love how,
I love what they're doing

00:08:12.813 --> 00:08:14.222
without him and I agree with you.

00:08:14.222 --> 00:08:16.833
Sometimes creators are
too close to the material

00:08:16.833 --> 00:08:19.378
and Shane you brought
up the film 'Watchmen.'

00:08:19.378 --> 00:08:21.235
64% on the Tomatometer.

00:08:21.235 --> 00:08:22.068
- [Shane] That feels [right].

00:08:22.068 --> 00:08:24.666
- 71% Audience Score,
Roxy I know you love it.

00:08:24.666 --> 00:08:27.140
But has over a million audience reviews.

00:08:27.140 --> 00:08:28.772
So I know a lot of people feel

00:08:28.772 --> 00:08:30.381
some type of way about the film.

00:08:30.381 --> 00:08:32.496
Personally, I love that
they made it because

00:08:32.496 --> 00:08:35.371
I don't feel like one film can encapsulate

00:08:35.371 --> 00:08:37.605
the magicness of 'Watchmen.'

00:08:37.605 --> 00:08:40.197
And I think you need a series to do that.

00:08:40.197 --> 00:08:42.943
- I think a series is a better
format to do that was well.

00:08:42.943 --> 00:08:46.191
But I think that the movie
was true to the graphic novel,

00:08:46.191 --> 00:08:48.088
which is why I liked
it and it's surprising

00:08:48.088 --> 00:08:51.767
that Alan Moore does not,
because it's such a dead ringer.

00:08:51.767 --> 00:08:53.903
I mean shot-for-shot most of the time.

00:08:53.903 --> 00:08:56.220
Yes, we do have some
changes like the ending

00:08:56.220 --> 00:08:58.190
where it's an explosion
as opposed to the squid

00:08:58.190 --> 00:09:01.360
but they're, for the
most part, it's an homage

00:09:01.360 --> 00:09:02.601
in the best kind of way.

00:09:02.601 --> 00:09:03.524
So that's why it worked for me.

00:09:03.524 --> 00:09:04.808
- Yeah, I love that you
brought up the ending

00:09:04.808 --> 00:09:05.973
because I wanted to bring that back

00:09:05.973 --> 00:09:08.186
to people maybe watching
this show right now.

00:09:08.186 --> 00:09:10.111
Can we kind of explain
the squid storm Shane

00:09:10.111 --> 00:09:11.598
that we were talking about before,

00:09:11.598 --> 00:09:14.107
because if you watch the
movie, didn't read the comic,

00:09:14.107 --> 00:09:15.332
you probably don't know what's
going on with the squids.

00:09:15.332 --> 00:09:16.623
- [Shan] You're like why squids?

00:09:16.623 --> 00:09:17.606
- [Naz] Yeah, exactly.

00:09:17.606 --> 00:09:18.439
- [Ryan] Going to Squidtown.

00:09:18.439 --> 00:09:21.085
- Yeah in the comic instead
of setting off some sort of,

00:09:21.085 --> 00:09:23.206
it was like tied to Manhattan.

00:09:23.206 --> 00:09:25.230
They set off this bomb.

00:09:25.230 --> 00:09:28.397
In the comic they transport this squid

00:09:29.295 --> 00:09:30.971
and make it look like an extraterrestrial

00:09:30.971 --> 00:09:32.209
from another planet.

00:09:32.209 --> 00:09:33.646
Like we're being invaded.

00:09:33.646 --> 00:09:34.684
It lands in New York.

00:09:34.684 --> 00:09:36.346
It's incredibly graphic and bloody.

00:09:36.346 --> 00:09:37.643
(Ryan laughs)

00:09:37.643 --> 00:09:39.123
- Yeah, which is why
there's a lot of predictions

00:09:39.123 --> 00:09:40.905
that the squids now falling from the sky

00:09:40.905 --> 00:09:44.789
is kind of a never forget
this is what can happen

00:09:44.789 --> 00:09:47.167
based off the graphic novel not the movie.

00:09:47.167 --> 00:09:49.120
- But is it a continuation of the hoax

00:09:49.120 --> 00:09:51.821
or is this residual from the transporting

00:09:51.821 --> 00:09:54.459
of I guess a giant squid that
was created by a biologist?

00:09:54.459 --> 00:09:55.682
- See Ryan, I'm with you.

00:09:55.682 --> 00:09:57.881
I feel like now the government's in on it.

00:09:57.881 --> 00:09:59.972
But we'll get to theories in a second.

00:09:59.972 --> 00:10:02.283
- I want to talk to you guys
about the standout performances

00:10:02.283 --> 00:10:04.472
'cause whether you watched,
whether you read the novel,

00:10:04.472 --> 00:10:07.515
watched the movie, you
like it or don't like it.

00:10:07.515 --> 00:10:09.260
The standout performances are unreal.

00:10:09.260 --> 00:10:10.231
We have three Oscar winners,

00:10:10.231 --> 00:10:12.873
Regina King, Jeremy
Irons, Louis Gossett Jr.

00:10:12.873 --> 00:10:14.185
So what have been your favorite moments

00:10:14.185 --> 00:10:16.232
or performances so far?

00:10:16.232 --> 00:10:19.388
- I love any time they cut
to Veidt and Jeremy Irons

00:10:19.388 --> 00:10:22.063
because I have no idea
what's going on with him.

00:10:22.063 --> 00:10:24.181
Which is similar to the
comic where for the first

00:10:24.181 --> 00:10:25.836
two thirds of the comic, you're like,

00:10:25.836 --> 00:10:27.466
well, what is the man doing?

00:10:27.466 --> 00:10:28.765
- It's very strange.

00:10:28.765 --> 00:10:31.202
He's like on his own little
'British Baking Show' island.

00:10:31.202 --> 00:10:32.762
- (laughs) He's in Wales.

00:10:32.762 --> 00:10:35.168
- And he's like reenacting
plays that he wrote.

00:10:35.168 --> 00:10:37.767
And I was like, it's basically
like just a continuation

00:10:37.767 --> 00:10:40.511
of his own ego which is very telling

00:10:40.511 --> 00:10:41.814
of his character, right?

00:10:41.814 --> 00:10:45.581
- He's incredible but
for me it is Regina King,

00:10:45.581 --> 00:10:47.734
Regina King, Regina King.

00:10:47.734 --> 00:10:49.927
She is so baller.

00:10:49.927 --> 00:10:53.451
The way that she even rolls
onto beds, grabs the gun.

00:10:53.451 --> 00:10:55.706
Like everything she
does she kicks butt in.

00:10:55.706 --> 00:10:58.985
But she's also this beautiful,
wonderful family woman.

00:10:58.985 --> 00:11:00.164
I just think that the way

00:11:00.164 --> 00:11:02.678
that they're depicting her is perfect.

00:11:02.678 --> 00:11:03.511
- I agree with you.

00:11:03.511 --> 00:11:05.498
She's a perfect balance
of strength and warmth

00:11:05.498 --> 00:11:07.756
and she makes me want
to be a nun in some way.

00:11:07.756 --> 00:11:09.312
I'm like my mom would be so proud.

00:11:09.312 --> 00:11:12.166
- This week when it was the
Christmas scene with her

00:11:12.166 --> 00:11:14.117
and her husband and
she just switches from,

00:11:14.117 --> 00:11:16.422
it was like just a really
wonderful tender scene.

00:11:16.422 --> 00:11:17.255
And I was like this is great.

00:11:17.255 --> 00:11:19.011
And then she just switches,
into like there's someone

00:11:19.011 --> 00:11:19.844
in the house.

00:11:19.844 --> 00:11:21.167
- Yeah and she's like
crawling in the kitchen

00:11:21.167 --> 00:11:22.068
and going behind.

00:11:22.068 --> 00:11:22.901
Yeah, so so good.

00:11:22.901 --> 00:11:24.768
I really enjoyed Tim Blake
Nelson's performance.

00:11:24.768 --> 00:11:25.967
- I was just about to say.

00:11:25.967 --> 00:11:27.662
- Looking Glass I think is my new

00:11:27.662 --> 00:11:29.076
favorite Watchmen superhero.

00:11:29.076 --> 00:11:33.206
And that interrogation scene
in the pod was so sick.

00:11:33.206 --> 00:11:34.064
He's so funny too.

00:11:34.064 --> 00:11:35.682
- The second interrogation
scene, which I guess

00:11:35.682 --> 00:11:37.043
isn't one but it is.

00:11:37.043 --> 00:11:39.257
When he's talking to
Regina King in the car,

00:11:39.257 --> 00:11:40.385
when he just speaks with

00:11:40.385 --> 00:11:42.505
that almost robotic, even-keel voice.

00:11:42.505 --> 00:11:43.851
It's pretty unsettling.

00:11:43.851 --> 00:11:44.866
- And he's like that is how

00:11:44.866 --> 00:11:46.235
I am crying under here or whatever.

00:11:46.235 --> 00:11:47.068
It's so good.

00:11:47.068 --> 00:11:48.972
But I agree with you guys: Jeremy Irons,

00:11:48.972 --> 00:11:51.282
every time I'm watching I'm
like, what the f--k is going on?

00:11:51.282 --> 00:11:53.796
And I have so many theories,
which is why I want to get

00:11:53.796 --> 00:11:56.293
into theories but really
quick we don't know

00:11:56.293 --> 00:11:57.945
the name of Jeremy Irons' character.

00:11:57.945 --> 00:12:00.267
So is he definitely Ozymandias?

00:12:00.267 --> 00:12:02.444
- He's Adrian Veidt, I think

00:12:02.444 --> 00:12:03.277
- I think he is too.

00:12:03.277 --> 00:12:04.661
- We think right, we don't know yet.

00:12:04.661 --> 00:12:07.452
- There was a headline that
said, "Adrian Veidt is Dead,"

00:12:07.452 --> 00:12:10.585
on one of the newspapers right.

00:12:10.585 --> 00:12:13.215
He is for sure.
- Has to be.

00:12:13.215 --> 00:12:14.224
- Without a doubt.

00:12:14.224 --> 00:12:15.993
- And the purple and
yellow cake I was like,

00:12:15.993 --> 00:12:16.959
okay this is him.

00:12:16.959 --> 00:12:17.792
- If he's not him

00:12:17.792 --> 00:12:19.551
why are they showing us
this man's birthday party

00:12:19.551 --> 00:12:20.418
three different times?

00:12:20.418 --> 00:12:21.780
Very weird choice.

00:12:21.780 --> 00:12:23.151
- (laughs) His anniversary yeah.

00:12:23.151 --> 00:12:25.572
- [Roxy] Or how does he
know to put on this play?

00:12:25.572 --> 00:12:26.992
How does he know what happened?

00:12:26.992 --> 00:12:29.533
- I feel like I would
not be utilizing you guys

00:12:29.533 --> 00:12:32.690
to the best of my ability
discuss all the Easter Eggs

00:12:32.690 --> 00:12:33.856
we've seen thus far.

00:12:33.856 --> 00:12:36.622
- I will stay when "Unforgettable"
starting playing with--

00:12:36.622 --> 00:12:38.356
- Don't put your fingers
together, what are you doing?

00:12:38.356 --> 00:12:40.436
Yeah, we're going to do this
for each of our Easter Eggs.

00:12:40.436 --> 00:12:42.027
- I'm talking like Steven Spielberg,

00:12:42.027 --> 00:12:44.697
he does does every interview by doing this

00:12:44.697 --> 00:12:47.329
and then he'll do that kind of stuff.

00:12:47.329 --> 00:12:49.803
When "Unforgettable" started
playing when he was walking

00:12:49.803 --> 00:12:50.889
around to get some more wine.

00:12:50.889 --> 00:12:52.679
I was like, this guy's going
to get sniped in his own house.

00:12:52.679 --> 00:12:54.288
Judd, who is who I'm talking about.

00:12:54.288 --> 00:12:56.944
There was a palpable sense
of dread this entire episode.

00:12:56.944 --> 00:12:58.636
When at any time he was alone, I was like

00:12:58.636 --> 00:13:01.674
this guy's gonna die
and sure enough he died.

00:13:01.674 --> 00:13:04.359
I'm not saying I'm a
prophet, but it happened.

00:13:04.359 --> 00:13:06.155
- (laughs) I'm not
saying I'm Dr. Manhattan.

00:13:06.155 --> 00:13:08.704
Well, why is "Unforgettable" an Easter Egg

00:13:08.704 --> 00:13:09.744
though for people who haven't seen it?

00:13:09.744 --> 00:13:13.230
- Oh in the film 'Watchmen,'
"Unforgettable" plays

00:13:13.230 --> 00:13:15.379
when The Comedian gets murked.

00:13:15.379 --> 00:13:17.581
- Yeah, most perfect use
of the song "Unforgettable"

00:13:17.581 --> 00:13:19.247
by the way.

00:13:19.247 --> 00:13:20.865
In opening of the film,
the one part I loved

00:13:20.865 --> 00:13:21.984
about the movie. (laughs)

00:13:21.984 --> 00:13:24.837
But "Unforgettable" is also
in the graphic novel, no?

00:13:24.837 --> 00:13:26.755
It's played when Ozymandias'
Nostalgia perfume

00:13:26.755 --> 00:13:28.008
commercials are on.

00:13:28.008 --> 00:13:29.769
- [All Three] Oh!

00:13:29.769 --> 00:13:31.123
- Come on guys!

00:13:31.123 --> 00:13:31.956
- You got it.

00:13:31.956 --> 00:13:33.152
- Yeah, I feel so fool right now.

00:13:33.152 --> 00:13:35.424
- I forgot he had that
funny perfume commercial.

00:13:35.424 --> 00:13:37.212
- What are some other Easter eggs?

00:13:37.212 --> 00:13:38.811
- There are also a ton of owls.

00:13:38.811 --> 00:13:41.319
Like through episode one and two,

00:13:41.319 --> 00:13:45.013
there's an owl mug, there
are kids dressed as owls,

00:13:45.013 --> 00:13:49.178
there's the homage to the ship in the sky.

00:13:49.178 --> 00:13:53.443
All different owl references
based off of Night Owl,

00:13:53.443 --> 00:13:56.732
the character from both the
movie and graphic novel.

00:13:56.732 --> 00:13:58.317
- Right and there have been two Night Owls

00:13:58.317 --> 00:13:59.972
in the universe just so everyone knows

00:13:59.972 --> 00:14:02.814
and Hollis Mason, correct
was the first one.

00:14:02.814 --> 00:14:03.647
- And Dan.

00:14:03.647 --> 00:14:04.498
- Dan Dreiberg was the second one.

00:14:04.498 --> 00:14:08.639
I thought Don Jonson's
character Judd Crawford,

00:14:08.639 --> 00:14:10.186
not Judd Apatow (laughs).

00:14:10.186 --> 00:14:11.064
- [Roxy] Was gonna be a third one?

00:14:11.064 --> 00:14:14.733
- Is, I think he's Dan because
he had 'Under the Hood'

00:14:14.733 --> 00:14:15.637
book on his desk.

00:14:15.637 --> 00:14:16.470
- He did.

00:14:16.470 --> 00:14:17.969
- Which is written by the first Night Owl.

00:14:17.969 --> 00:14:18.806
- (whispers) Interesting.

00:14:18.806 --> 00:14:21.437
- Do we know the whereabout
of Dan does it say on that--

00:14:21.437 --> 00:14:22.874
- But he also was in Archie,

00:14:22.874 --> 00:14:25.032
which is the name of the Owl ship.

00:14:25.032 --> 00:14:26.789
So I'm like, is this Dan Dreiberg?

00:14:26.789 --> 00:14:27.622
- Sup theory.

00:14:27.622 --> 00:14:28.874
- Thanks guys.

00:14:28.874 --> 00:14:32.722
- There's been a few also,
the ending of the pilot,

00:14:32.722 --> 00:14:34.630
with the blood drip onto the--

00:14:34.630 --> 00:14:35.883
- [Ryan] Classic.

00:14:35.883 --> 00:14:37.872
- I can't believe that did not occur to me

00:14:37.872 --> 00:14:41.212
that they would try to (laughs)
like put that visual cue

00:14:41.212 --> 00:14:43.776
in there 'cause when it
happened I was like, whoa.

00:14:43.776 --> 00:14:46.106
I got a little shiver when it happened.

00:14:46.106 --> 00:14:48.618
- Why is it so profound to
people who haven't read?

00:14:48.618 --> 00:14:50.570
- Because when The
Comedian dies in the movie

00:14:50.570 --> 00:14:53.814
and in the comic book, he
falls out of the window.

00:14:53.814 --> 00:14:54.647
- [Roxy] And there's a pin.

00:14:54.647 --> 00:14:55.869
- I don't know if we can show it.

00:14:55.869 --> 00:14:59.025
His pin falls off, no,
we'll leave it there.

00:14:59.025 --> 00:14:59.858
- Alan Moore will hate us.

00:14:59.858 --> 00:15:00.691
- He'll kill us.

00:15:00.691 --> 00:15:02.597
- I already imitated him.

00:15:02.597 --> 00:15:03.467
(Naz laughs)

00:15:03.467 --> 00:15:04.698
I'm already on his crap list.

00:15:04.698 --> 00:15:07.132
- So he wears that iconic
little smiley face pin.

00:15:07.132 --> 00:15:09.420
It falls down out of the window after him

00:15:09.420 --> 00:15:11.336
and a little drop of
blood just specks on it

00:15:11.336 --> 00:15:14.544
and becomes sort of a visual
motif for the whole story.

00:15:14.544 --> 00:15:17.541
- And this was on the
police badge was it not.

00:15:17.541 --> 00:15:20.409
So that one drop of blood,
it was a little different

00:15:20.409 --> 00:15:21.477
but a similar drop.

00:15:21.477 --> 00:15:24.515
- And I think The Comedian
to the smiley face as a badge

00:15:24.515 --> 00:15:25.598
not as a pin.

00:15:26.868 --> 00:15:29.385
Apparently pins are not that badass.

00:15:29.385 --> 00:15:31.289
- Blood drops in this universe
have a mind of their own.

00:15:31.289 --> 00:15:32.122
(Naz laughs)

00:15:32.122 --> 00:15:33.466
Very serendipitous the way they fall.

00:15:33.466 --> 00:15:36.004
- What about the newsstand?

00:15:36.004 --> 00:15:41.004
- That's interesting, I
wonder what they're getting at

00:15:41.075 --> 00:15:44.062
and how they're gonna use
that to tell different stories

00:15:44.062 --> 00:15:46.231
throughout the first season of this

00:15:46.231 --> 00:15:48.153
or possibly only season I've heard.

00:15:48.153 --> 00:15:50.210
- In the book it was always
just sort of a weird glimpse

00:15:50.210 --> 00:15:52.912
into just the common folk.

00:15:52.912 --> 00:15:55.770
'Cause they would just cut to
conversations between them.

00:15:55.770 --> 00:15:58.484
- And then we showed the actually
comic that he was reading.

00:15:58.484 --> 00:16:01.369
- Yeah the pirate comics,
which in this feels like

00:16:01.369 --> 00:16:04.238
American Hero story is
like their embedded story.

00:16:04.238 --> 00:16:05.601
So I hope we see more.

00:16:05.601 --> 00:16:07.975
I was glad we saw more
of it in episode two.

00:16:07.975 --> 00:16:09.188
I wonder if they're
going to continue that.

00:16:09.188 --> 00:16:11.183
- Another, I don't know
if it's an Easter Egg

00:16:11.183 --> 00:16:13.366
or a theory but I feel
like there a lot of twists

00:16:13.366 --> 00:16:15.261
and turns in the graphic novel

00:16:15.261 --> 00:16:17.866
and when we see that
Regina King's character

00:16:17.866 --> 00:16:20.724
has been shot, the next thing that we see

00:16:20.724 --> 00:16:23.657
is her in the hospital with somebody

00:16:23.657 --> 00:16:26.338
who possibly could have
shot her if he is a bad guy.

00:16:26.338 --> 00:16:27.171
- [Naz] Judd Crawford.

00:16:27.171 --> 00:16:29.747
- Exactly, so there are a
lot of moments like that

00:16:29.747 --> 00:16:33.145
in the movie and in the
novel where the next image

00:16:33.145 --> 00:16:35.643
you see is actually the
answer to the question

00:16:35.643 --> 00:16:36.476
you've been asking.

00:16:36.476 --> 00:16:38.541
- Wow, that's a really good Easter Egg

00:16:38.541 --> 00:16:41.850
and I think you're right
because when Angela

00:16:41.850 --> 00:16:45.075
asks Judd, "Did everyone
or did you get shot too?"

00:16:45.075 --> 00:16:46.608
I feel like he looks at his shoulder

00:16:46.608 --> 00:16:48.367
and he's like, "Everyone got hurt."

00:16:48.367 --> 00:16:49.800
He doesn't really answer
the question there.

00:16:49.800 --> 00:16:51.572
- And he said everyone else died

00:16:51.572 --> 00:16:54.405
except of us, so maybe he shot her

00:16:55.419 --> 00:16:57.598
and for some reason has
a soft spot for her.

00:16:57.598 --> 00:16:59.768
So brought her to the hospital.

00:16:59.768 --> 00:17:00.601
- So let's get into that.

00:17:00.601 --> 00:17:02.094
What are some theories and predictions

00:17:02.094 --> 00:17:04.192
that you guys have for
this show moving forward

00:17:04.192 --> 00:17:06.564
or flashbacks or characters
that you want to see?

00:17:06.564 --> 00:17:10.198
- I'm super focused on Hooded
Justice and who that is

00:17:10.198 --> 00:17:11.488
and what's happening.

00:17:11.488 --> 00:17:15.017
In the second episode
with Muller, the woman

00:17:15.017 --> 00:17:17.217
that we see who goes and
transcribes in English.

00:17:17.217 --> 00:17:22.064
That last name is who Hollis
is referencing in the novel

00:17:22.064 --> 00:17:25.363
could possibly be Hooded
Justice but maybe a relative

00:17:25.363 --> 00:17:27.688
because it's a boy or is it Will?

00:17:27.688 --> 00:17:30.310
I have so many theories
about who is under that hood.

00:17:30.310 --> 00:17:32.847
- [Naz] A lot of people
think it's Will Reeves.

00:17:32.847 --> 00:17:34.812
Do you guys think so because
he wears a lot of red

00:17:34.812 --> 00:17:38.046
and the rope that's used
to hang Judd is similar

00:17:38.046 --> 00:17:39.321
to the rope that he had?

00:17:39.321 --> 00:17:43.190
- Possibly, I know that
we see in the TV show

00:17:43.190 --> 00:17:45.368
that it's a white man under that hood

00:17:45.368 --> 00:17:47.896
but that could be history.

00:17:47.896 --> 00:17:51.026
- [Shane] That's also like
the TV show within the show.

00:17:51.026 --> 00:17:52.587
- [Naz] 'American Hero Story.'

00:17:52.587 --> 00:17:54.346
- It could be just some
whitewashing commentary.

00:17:54.346 --> 00:17:55.752
- Yeah commentary on whitewashing.

00:17:55.752 --> 00:17:58.423
- I thought it was like
the Black Freighter.

00:17:58.423 --> 00:18:00.260
You know how the comic was within a comic.

00:18:00.260 --> 00:18:02.502
- That's for sure what
they're doing with it.

00:18:02.502 --> 00:18:03.577
- I think it could be Will.

00:18:03.577 --> 00:18:05.291
I'll go out on a limb there.

00:18:05.291 --> 00:18:06.173
I think it could be Will.

00:18:06.173 --> 00:18:07.987
- What are some other
theories you guys have

00:18:07.987 --> 00:18:09.348
or stuff that you'd like to see happen?

00:18:09.348 --> 00:18:10.611
- I'm really curious
how they're gonna work

00:18:10.611 --> 00:18:14.031
Dr. Manhattan into it because
they had that one little clip

00:18:14.031 --> 00:18:16.781
of him just being on Mars
like tearing his castle,

00:18:16.781 --> 00:18:18.294
his little palace down.

00:18:18.294 --> 00:18:19.757
I was like, what's he up to?

00:18:19.757 --> 00:18:21.477
- I think there's no way he's on Mars.

00:18:21.477 --> 00:18:22.328
- He's back right?

00:18:22.328 --> 00:18:24.763
- There's no way he's on
Mars because we've referenced

00:18:24.763 --> 00:18:27.279
it 50 times, he's on Mars, he's on Mars.

00:18:27.279 --> 00:18:28.692
If he was actually on
Mars why do we keep saying

00:18:28.692 --> 00:18:29.525
he's on Mars?

00:18:29.525 --> 00:18:30.991
- And you know what else
they reference a lot?

00:18:30.991 --> 00:18:33.450
They say that he doesn't have the power

00:18:33.450 --> 00:18:35.191
to shapeshift into someone else.

00:18:35.191 --> 00:18:38.041
So it makes me think that maybe
Ozymandias or Jeremy Irons'

00:18:38.041 --> 00:18:40.008
character, you know all
those clones he has?

00:18:40.008 --> 00:18:43.626
Maybe Dr. Manhattan is his tinkering?

00:18:43.626 --> 00:18:45.980
Because the castle kind
of looks like his house.

00:18:45.980 --> 00:18:50.628
- Reenacting the whole
accident that turned him

00:18:50.628 --> 00:18:53.878
into Dr. Manhattan, I wonder if that's,

00:18:55.020 --> 00:18:56.282
yeah, it could be him.

00:18:56.282 --> 00:18:58.003
Can you actually explain
that and go into that

00:18:58.003 --> 00:18:59.883
for people who haven't
read the novel or the movie

00:18:59.883 --> 00:19:01.959
and what that play was,
'The Watchmaker's Son.'

00:19:01.959 --> 00:19:04.921
- So Veidt, if it is Veidt
who I said, Jeremy Irons'

00:19:04.921 --> 00:19:07.656
character is reenacting
the initial accident

00:19:07.656 --> 00:19:11.042
that created Dr. Manhattan
when we got caught

00:19:11.042 --> 00:19:15.106
inside a testing chamber
and I don't know why

00:19:15.106 --> 00:19:16.707
he's so obsessed with it.

00:19:16.707 --> 00:19:18.251
- He's very method with recreating it.

00:19:18.251 --> 00:19:19.084
- Yes he is.

00:19:19.084 --> 00:19:20.093
- He sets a man on fire.

00:19:20.093 --> 00:19:21.348
- His writing's so terrible

00:19:21.348 --> 00:19:22.777
and he's like, I need real tears!

00:19:22.777 --> 00:19:25.943
- I love when the other clone, he's like,

00:19:25.943 --> 00:19:28.288
you'll be him now and
the guy was like, yeah.

00:19:28.288 --> 00:19:30.384
Like I know, you're
gonna burn up tomorrow.

00:19:30.384 --> 00:19:31.828
- Burn alive.

00:19:31.828 --> 00:19:34.458
- That was so wild to watch
because as I'm watching

00:19:34.458 --> 00:19:36.446
I'm thinking there is no way

00:19:36.446 --> 00:19:38.469
he's actually burning a person.

00:19:38.469 --> 00:19:42.112
These are great special effects
or he's got these robots.

00:19:42.112 --> 00:19:43.070
He went there.

00:19:43.070 --> 00:19:44.193
- [Ryan] And he opens the door

00:19:44.193 --> 00:19:45.506
and the guy was Kentucky-fried.

00:19:45.506 --> 00:19:47.137
He was definitely burnt to a crisp.

00:19:47.137 --> 00:19:50.156
- Straight up, so they're
reenacting that scene

00:19:50.156 --> 00:19:52.386
showing how he got to be.

00:19:52.386 --> 00:19:55.374
Which is kind of great for
people who didn't read the novel

00:19:55.374 --> 00:19:56.723
or who have never seen the movie

00:19:56.723 --> 00:19:58.385
because now they know
what the origin story is.

00:19:58.385 --> 00:20:00.219
- Also low stakes by the way.

00:20:00.219 --> 00:20:01.655
I do love the moment of him,

00:20:01.655 --> 00:20:03.542
me and Shane talked about this before.

00:20:03.542 --> 00:20:08.251
That shot of Dr. Manhattan
just destroying his castle

00:20:08.251 --> 00:20:10.446
like a kid playing with Tinker Toys.

00:20:10.446 --> 00:20:12.570
It just cuts to this very ambient shot,

00:20:12.570 --> 00:20:13.622
there's no noise.

00:20:13.622 --> 00:20:15.703
It's just him knocking stuff down on Mars.

00:20:15.703 --> 00:20:17.436
Like he's just bored up there.

00:20:17.436 --> 00:20:19.109
- And for everyone
watching just so you know,

00:20:19.109 --> 00:20:21.339
we've only see two episodes so far

00:20:21.339 --> 00:20:23.366
and the other big thing that
we learned in the episode

00:20:23.366 --> 00:20:26.383
last night was that Will
is Angela's grandfather.

00:20:26.383 --> 00:20:30.375
So where do we think that
storyline's gonna go?

00:20:30.375 --> 00:20:33.552
- I think it's difficult
because right now she,

00:20:33.552 --> 00:20:36.196
people don't even know
that she's a police officer

00:20:36.196 --> 00:20:38.330
and then they don't know
that she has this other

00:20:38.330 --> 00:20:40.848
secret identity as well.

00:20:40.848 --> 00:20:42.379
And now it's her family.

00:20:42.379 --> 00:20:44.557
So she's already protecting
him before she knows

00:20:44.557 --> 00:20:46.024
that it's her grandfather.

00:20:46.024 --> 00:20:49.473
Now that she knows that,
she's not gonna turn him in.

00:20:49.473 --> 00:20:51.214
And we don't even know if he is the one

00:20:51.214 --> 00:20:53.258
who hung the Chief of Police even though

00:20:53.258 --> 00:20:55.753
he asks her, do you think
I could lift 200 pounds?

00:20:55.753 --> 00:20:59.611
So I think that she's, they're
gonna team up in some way.

00:20:59.611 --> 00:21:02.034
- It was pretty obvious the hiding spot

00:21:02.034 --> 00:21:03.590
that they found the cloak.

00:21:03.590 --> 00:21:05.837
- Yeah, I wondered that.

00:21:05.837 --> 00:21:08.364
- When they found Don Johnson's cloak.

00:21:08.364 --> 00:21:10.696
- Yeah, Judd Crawford's,
I keep wanting to call him

00:21:10.696 --> 00:21:12.986
Judd Apatow and in my head
I'm like Judd Crawford.

00:21:12.986 --> 00:21:13.978
I don't know many Judds.

00:21:13.978 --> 00:21:16.359
- I think that is not his.

00:21:16.359 --> 00:21:18.273
- You think that it was placed there?

00:21:18.273 --> 00:21:19.649
- No, I do not think that.

00:21:19.649 --> 00:21:21.311
I think it's his father's.

00:21:21.311 --> 00:21:22.919
- Oh, 'cause that picture, oh!

00:21:22.919 --> 00:21:25.543
- I think a lot of this show
is about paying for sins

00:21:25.543 --> 00:21:28.140
of your family members
and the older generations

00:21:28.140 --> 00:21:31.056
and I think it's there
possibly as a reminder

00:21:31.056 --> 00:21:34.226
to him as what his family has done

00:21:34.226 --> 00:21:35.894
and how he has to fight against this.

00:21:35.894 --> 00:21:38.432
Because it would just be too obvious

00:21:38.432 --> 00:21:40.774
and too early of a reveal
to have him be that bad

00:21:40.774 --> 00:21:43.357
of a person and there's
so many other questions

00:21:43.357 --> 00:21:45.289
that would come up if that were really,

00:21:45.289 --> 00:21:47.687
if he's really a member of the KKK.

00:21:47.687 --> 00:21:52.135
If he really is this white
supremacist then why does

00:21:52.135 --> 00:21:53.529
he live his life like this?

00:21:53.529 --> 00:21:56.086
- Well the thing is he
has that painting up,

00:21:56.086 --> 00:21:57.594
which is the title of episode two,

00:21:57.594 --> 00:22:00.250
"Martial Feats of Comanche Horsemanship."

00:22:00.250 --> 00:22:03.571
I'm curious to know how is
this White Night reference

00:22:03.571 --> 00:22:06.792
going to be tied together,
Jeremy Irons' character rides

00:22:06.792 --> 00:22:10.094
a white horse, there's a
white horse in the painting.

00:22:10.094 --> 00:22:13.376
The White Night is another
name for the Tulsa Massacre.

00:22:13.376 --> 00:22:14.991
How is this all going to play together?

00:22:14.991 --> 00:22:17.863
- Speaking of which I'm
kinda curious how Rorschach

00:22:17.863 --> 00:22:21.196
became the figurehead for
white supremacy in the show.

00:22:21.196 --> 00:22:22.413
It feels accurate.

00:22:22.413 --> 00:22:23.246
- [Naz] I kinda get it.

00:22:23.246 --> 00:22:24.909
- [Ryan] I wonder if we're
gonna see any of that.

00:22:24.909 --> 00:22:26.537
- Why is that accurate to
people who haven't watched

00:22:26.537 --> 00:22:28.771
because he was ultra-violent in a way

00:22:28.771 --> 00:22:29.604
or extremist in the comic.

00:22:29.604 --> 00:22:30.437
- I guess that makes sense yeah.

00:22:30.437 --> 00:22:32.845
- I think just because he was an extremist

00:22:32.845 --> 00:22:36.082
and it seems like something
that someone would latch onto.

00:22:36.082 --> 00:22:38.649
(laughs) They're certainly misinterpreting

00:22:38.649 --> 00:22:42.316
a lot of his ideas but
he did send his diary

00:22:43.151 --> 00:22:45.837
to the newspaper at the end of the comic.

00:22:45.837 --> 00:22:48.307
- If Rorschach was alive
today, I don't think

00:22:48.307 --> 00:22:49.696
he would be a member of this group.

00:22:49.696 --> 00:22:53.553
I think based off of what he
stood for, people could read

00:22:53.553 --> 00:22:56.553
what he was saying and
interpret it this way.

00:22:56.553 --> 00:22:58.340
- Almost like a Rorschach blot.

00:22:58.340 --> 00:23:00.662
- [Naz] Yeah. (laughs)

00:23:00.662 --> 00:23:04.245
And if you guys actually
go on that Pdpedia

00:23:05.430 --> 00:23:07.147
that was telling you about,
I think they explained

00:23:07.147 --> 00:23:08.757
what happened in those 30 so years

00:23:08.757 --> 00:23:10.963
and how that mask evolved.

00:23:10.963 --> 00:23:15.288
You don't need to read it but
I hope that they explain this.

00:23:15.288 --> 00:23:16.528
Yeah, it's like a PDF.

00:23:16.528 --> 00:23:17.361
- Good to know.

00:23:17.361 --> 00:23:18.732
- I hope that they
explain this in the show.

00:23:18.732 --> 00:23:21.697
But I'm curious to know when
you guys read 'Watchmen'

00:23:21.697 --> 00:23:23.724
graphic novel I feel like
everyone kind of sat around

00:23:23.724 --> 00:23:26.184
their couch and they were like,
who do you relate to more?

00:23:26.184 --> 00:23:28.370
And for a lot of people
it was Rorschach, right?

00:23:28.370 --> 00:23:29.933
- I read it when I was in college

00:23:29.933 --> 00:23:31.331
and I was like, yeah, he's cool.

00:23:31.331 --> 00:23:33.301
But in retrospect as I've grown up.

00:23:33.301 --> 00:23:36.413
I'm like that guy had
some issues. (laughs)

00:23:36.413 --> 00:23:38.842
- Had some issues so how do
you feel about his iconic mask

00:23:38.842 --> 00:23:41.083
now being the face of the 7th Calvary?

00:23:41.083 --> 00:23:43.993
- When I read this there
was nobody that I was like,

00:23:43.993 --> 00:23:45.548
I want to grow up to be like that person.

00:23:45.548 --> 00:23:46.972
- [Shane] (laughs) They
all got some issues.

00:23:46.972 --> 00:23:48.210
- I wanted to be Dr. Manhattan.

00:23:48.210 --> 00:23:49.043
- Did you?

00:23:50.590 --> 00:23:52.560
- Cold, emotionless.

00:23:52.560 --> 00:23:54.482
- Just not wearing clothes.

00:23:54.482 --> 00:23:56.841
- I mean that would be kind
of cool but so emotionless.

00:23:56.841 --> 00:23:59.875
You don't want to be
sleeping with somebody

00:23:59.875 --> 00:24:01.811
while you're working on your work outside.

00:24:01.811 --> 00:24:03.670
- He has such a good memory in the comic,

00:24:03.670 --> 00:24:05.860
he's like in 1959 I was doing this

00:24:05.860 --> 00:24:07.718
and he's like on November
8th I was doing this.

00:24:07.718 --> 00:24:09.142
I'm like that would be a
really great skill to have.

00:24:09.142 --> 00:24:10.937
- Well, there were aspects
of each of the characters

00:24:10.937 --> 00:24:14.224
that you kind of wanted,
except for some of them.

00:24:14.224 --> 00:24:16.110
Like The Comedian I would
say outright probably

00:24:16.110 --> 00:24:17.300
none of us wanted wanted
to be any part of.

00:24:17.300 --> 00:24:19.300
- That guy we all agree.

00:24:20.256 --> 00:24:22.002
- Weird sense of humor.

00:24:22.002 --> 00:24:24.176
- I like the references to Rorschach

00:24:24.176 --> 00:24:28.376
and people who are looking
at either the Minutemen

00:24:28.376 --> 00:24:30.838
or the Watchmen and pulling
different traits from them

00:24:30.838 --> 00:24:32.396
and that's the start for this series.

00:24:32.396 --> 00:24:34.482
- What are some other
shows on TV that you think

00:24:34.482 --> 00:24:36.861
people should check out if
you they're into 'Watchmen'?

00:24:36.861 --> 00:24:38.418
So shows like 'Watchmen.'

00:24:38.418 --> 00:24:41.169
- I think you're into
'Watchmen' a show that I love

00:24:41.169 --> 00:24:43.214
that people have not been really talking

00:24:43.214 --> 00:24:44.863
about is 'Doom Patrol.'

00:24:44.863 --> 00:24:46.758
Now, it's a DC property.

00:24:46.758 --> 00:24:48.986
So if you like 'Watchmen,'
it's a comic book property

00:24:48.986 --> 00:24:51.492
by DC and it's weird.

00:24:51.492 --> 00:24:54.421
So in that aspect it's
kind of similar because

00:24:54.421 --> 00:24:58.298
it's very offbeat like
squid falling from the sky,

00:24:58.298 --> 00:25:00.305
that kind of stuff happens on the show

00:25:00.305 --> 00:25:01.447
all of the time.

00:25:01.447 --> 00:25:03.977
And it's also got that hero
aspect where the heroes

00:25:03.977 --> 00:25:06.961
are not the best people on the planet.

00:25:06.961 --> 00:25:10.565
They definitely have more
layers to their onions

00:25:10.565 --> 00:25:13.165
and like I said it's off, it's strange.

00:25:13.165 --> 00:25:16.656
It's on DC Universe and if you
want to be part of something

00:25:16.656 --> 00:25:20.081
that seems really weird but also kick butt

00:25:20.081 --> 00:25:22.152
then 'Doom Patrol' is where it's at.

00:25:22.152 --> 00:25:22.985
- I'm sold.

00:25:22.985 --> 00:25:23.818
Shane what about you?

00:25:23.818 --> 00:25:25.655
- I'd go with 'Leftovers,'

00:25:25.655 --> 00:25:27.739
which is Damon Lindelof's show before.

00:25:27.739 --> 00:25:29.024
It was also HBO.

00:25:29.024 --> 00:25:30.613
Roxy you were saying you were a fan.

00:25:30.613 --> 00:25:32.010
- [Roxy] Massive, massive love that show.

00:25:32.010 --> 00:25:32.843
- It's incredible.

00:25:32.843 --> 00:25:35.935
I think it's very handy for
people watching Watchmen

00:25:35.935 --> 00:25:40.060
because you sort of get a feel
for how he approaches stories

00:25:40.060 --> 00:25:43.627
'cause he always comes at
stuff from a weird angle.

00:25:43.627 --> 00:25:44.913
- Flashbacks as well.

00:25:44.913 --> 00:25:46.156
- So many flashbacks.

00:25:46.156 --> 00:25:48.202
He sort of took everything
he learned from Lost,

00:25:48.202 --> 00:25:49.962
I know a lot of people have
some beefs with that show,

00:25:49.962 --> 00:25:52.712
I love it and improved upon them.

00:25:53.902 --> 00:25:56.245
And when I heard he was
doing 'Watchmen,' I was like,

00:25:56.245 --> 00:25:57.274
yeah, perfect.

00:25:57.274 --> 00:25:59.071
- And I think I've heard
through the rumor mill,

00:25:59.071 --> 00:26:01.531
that 'Leftovers,' you
know how certain episodes

00:26:01.531 --> 00:26:02.904
focus on one character?

00:26:02.904 --> 00:26:04.251
I heard that that's what's coming up

00:26:04.251 --> 00:26:06.545
or what we should look forward
to for 'Watchmen,' yeah.

00:26:06.545 --> 00:26:08.556
What about you Ry?

00:26:08.556 --> 00:26:11.740
- I would say, I guess
not in the sense of tone

00:26:11.740 --> 00:26:14.012
because the show I'm about
to suggest is not as strange

00:26:14.012 --> 00:26:17.369
as weird in 'Watchmen' is but
in terms of dystopian future,

00:26:17.369 --> 00:26:19.742
I would say, 'Man in the High Castle.'

00:26:19.742 --> 00:26:21.164
That's basically the
entire premise of that show

00:26:21.164 --> 00:26:24.504
is this alternate set of
futures and if you're into that

00:26:24.504 --> 00:26:26.247
sort of thing, it's a fun show.

00:26:26.247 --> 00:26:28.795
- I would say, check out 'The Boys.'

00:26:28.795 --> 00:26:31.500
'Cause I love seeing superheros
in this raw real human

00:26:31.500 --> 00:26:33.055
way which is why I love Watchmen

00:26:33.055 --> 00:26:35.568
and I think 'The Boys' does
that and season two comes out

00:26:35.568 --> 00:26:38.257
Summer 2020, so you have
time to catch up on it.

00:26:38.257 --> 00:26:40.741
So as major fans we can
obviously go on about 'Watchmen'

00:26:40.741 --> 00:26:41.781
all day, every day.

00:26:41.781 --> 00:26:43.030
Are you guys going to stick with it?

00:26:43.030 --> 00:26:44.743
Do you like where the
story's going so far?

00:26:44.743 --> 00:26:48.549
- Yeah, I'll watch it
until, as long as it's on

00:26:48.549 --> 00:26:49.779
I'm gonna watch it.

00:26:49.779 --> 00:26:52.392
I love Damon Lindelof, I love 'Watchmen.'

00:26:52.392 --> 00:26:53.363
I'm here.

00:26:53.363 --> 00:26:54.584
(Naz laughs)

00:26:54.584 --> 00:26:56.070
I'll be here to talk about
it whenever you want.

00:26:56.070 --> 00:26:57.497
- Yeah, I think I'll stick with it.

00:26:57.497 --> 00:26:58.330
I have a TV.

00:26:58.330 --> 00:27:01.483
So as long as I have a TV I'll watch it.

00:27:01.483 --> 00:27:03.406
- 100% I'm a Watchwoman now.

00:27:03.406 --> 00:27:06.077
This is definitely going to
be a show that I stay with

00:27:06.077 --> 00:27:08.444
no matter what even if
there are highs and lows

00:27:08.444 --> 00:27:11.382
because I feel like they
know what they're doing.

00:27:11.382 --> 00:27:13.447
They have and endgame
and I gotta get there.

00:27:13.447 --> 00:27:15.143
- Yeah, I'm so excited to see what happens

00:27:15.143 --> 00:27:17.815
because I heard that the
series gets so so good.

00:27:17.815 --> 00:27:20.834
But now it is time for our Fresh Picks.

00:27:20.834 --> 00:27:23.365
These are shows, doesn't
have to do with 'Watchmen,'

00:27:23.365 --> 00:27:25.188
just any show on TV that
you think people should

00:27:25.188 --> 00:27:26.517
add to their watch list.

00:27:26.517 --> 00:27:27.843
What are you guys watchin'?

00:27:27.843 --> 00:27:30.604
- 'On Becoming A God in Central Florida'!

00:27:30.604 --> 00:27:33.471
If you've never heard of
that then you're not alone.

00:27:33.471 --> 00:27:35.479
Because nobody knows what that show is.

00:27:35.479 --> 00:27:36.419
- [Naz] It's on Showtime, yes?

00:27:36.419 --> 00:27:38.117
- Yes, it's on Showtime,
it's Kirsten Dunst.

00:27:38.117 --> 00:27:41.040
It's weird, it is a pyramid-scheme show.

00:27:41.040 --> 00:27:42.828
And you're watching it all happen

00:27:42.828 --> 00:27:45.536
screaming at the television,
like, "Don't do it!"

00:27:45.536 --> 00:27:49.187
Every single episode, I
can't believe this is getting

00:27:49.187 --> 00:27:52.941
more buzz right now because it's awesome.

00:27:52.941 --> 00:27:54.091
It's really great.

00:27:54.091 --> 00:27:55.559
- Do you think it's
because it's on Showtime?

00:27:55.559 --> 00:27:57.091
Maybe it's had for people to find?

00:27:57.091 --> 00:28:01.408
- I don't know, there's
so much content now.

00:28:01.408 --> 00:28:03.076
There's so many TV shows

00:28:03.076 --> 00:28:06.647
that I think this might
be like 'Succession'

00:28:06.647 --> 00:28:08.737
in season two more people
are going to catch on to it

00:28:08.737 --> 00:28:11.487
if we get there, hopefully
enough people watch that we do.

00:28:11.487 --> 00:28:13.407
But if you are interested
in it, you'll know

00:28:13.407 --> 00:28:15.507
off the first episode if you like it.

00:28:15.507 --> 00:28:17.056
The way the first episode
ends you're either

00:28:17.056 --> 00:28:18.053
in or your out.

00:28:18.053 --> 00:28:22.701
I think the first episode
is for free on YouTube too.

00:28:22.701 --> 00:28:23.534
- From the YouTube king himself.

00:28:23.534 --> 00:28:24.917
(all laugh)

00:28:24.917 --> 00:28:25.750
- Shared crown.

00:28:25.750 --> 00:28:26.583
- Kings.

00:28:26.583 --> 00:28:27.416
- YTK.

00:28:27.416 --> 00:28:28.249
(Ryan laughs)

00:28:28.249 --> 00:28:30.017
- Sorry Ryan was OG.

00:28:30.017 --> 00:28:31.636
Ryan's like gonna kill me. (laughs)

00:28:31.636 --> 00:28:32.995
Shane, what's your Fresh Pick?

00:28:32.995 --> 00:28:36.198
- I would go with, it just
ended, 'Righteous Gemstones.'

00:28:36.198 --> 00:28:37.496
I heard a lot of people talking about it.

00:28:37.496 --> 00:28:40.790
I'm not always sold on
Danny McBride's stuff.

00:28:40.790 --> 00:28:42.427
So I approached it with caution.

00:28:42.427 --> 00:28:45.982
It's got the usual
incredibly vulgar vibe to it.

00:28:45.982 --> 00:28:48.930
But it really ended up surprising me.

00:28:48.930 --> 00:28:50.513
I wasn't totally sold from episode one

00:28:50.513 --> 00:28:52.882
but by the end of the season
it lands in a place that's

00:28:52.882 --> 00:28:55.049
surprisingly heartwarming.

00:28:57.036 --> 00:29:00.374
It's weird, it's the most
vulgar, insane, heartwarming

00:29:00.374 --> 00:29:01.843
show I've ever watched.

00:29:01.843 --> 00:29:02.676
- Is it funny?

00:29:02.676 --> 00:29:03.748
- It is very funny.

00:29:03.748 --> 00:29:06.787
Also there's a woman on
it named Edi Patterson,

00:29:06.787 --> 00:29:08.003
who I haven't seen in anything else.

00:29:08.003 --> 00:29:10.896
I think she wrote for SNL
and was on 'Vice Principals'

00:29:10.896 --> 00:29:12.468
but she is so funny.

00:29:12.468 --> 00:29:13.301
- [Naz] So good in that.

00:29:13.301 --> 00:29:15.038
- Check it out, you've sold me.

00:29:15.038 --> 00:29:16.815
- (shrugs) It's what I'm here to do.

00:29:16.815 --> 00:29:18.652
- There is a part of me that wants

00:29:18.652 --> 00:29:20.726
to say Great 'British
Baking Show' just because

00:29:20.726 --> 00:29:24.388
of Jeremy Irons' birthday cake island.

00:29:24.388 --> 00:29:25.252
But,

00:29:25.252 --> 00:29:26.085
(Naz laughs)

00:29:26.085 --> 00:29:30.277
I will say, I'm just gonna
say 'The Good Place.'

00:29:30.277 --> 00:29:33.016
Everyone knows 'The Good Place' is great.

00:29:33.016 --> 00:29:35.714
It's the last season,
you can watch it live.

00:29:35.714 --> 00:29:38.397
Not a lot of great shows are
actually live on television,

00:29:38.397 --> 00:29:40.688
where you can watch it
and it becomes an event.

00:29:40.688 --> 00:29:41.638
Get it while it's hot.

00:29:41.638 --> 00:29:43.091
- That's true, I like that.

00:29:43.091 --> 00:29:45.851
- I'm watching 'Living With Yourself.'

00:29:45.851 --> 00:29:48.202
Have you guys seen that
with Paul Rudd on Netflix?

00:29:48.202 --> 00:29:51.328
Paul Rudd playing Paul Rudd
and opposite Paul Rudd.

00:29:51.328 --> 00:29:53.041
- [Ryan] Oh yeah, I
saw that on a bus stop.

00:29:53.041 --> 00:29:54.456
- Yeah, it's really good.

00:29:54.456 --> 00:29:56.445
It's very easy to watch.

00:29:56.445 --> 00:29:58.688
The episodes are like 25 minutes long.

00:29:58.688 --> 00:30:01.302
There's only episodes,
there's a great cliffhanger

00:30:01.302 --> 00:30:02.911
at the end for season two.

00:30:02.911 --> 00:30:04.876
And Paul Rudd's just so likable and funny

00:30:04.876 --> 00:30:07.803
and there's a Tom Brady
cameo that's a little

00:30:07.803 --> 00:30:09.964
controversial right now
but that's all I can say.

00:30:09.964 --> 00:30:11.936
But it's definitely a reason to tune in.

00:30:11.936 --> 00:30:13.687
- You know what? I've got
my finger on the pulse

00:30:13.687 --> 00:30:16.835
of sports so well that when
I watched that first episode

00:30:16.835 --> 00:30:19.407
he showed up, I was
like, oh who's this guy?

00:30:19.407 --> 00:30:20.240
(Naz laughs)

00:30:20.240 --> 00:30:22.071
And then in the next
episode they mentioned

00:30:22.071 --> 00:30:22.904
it was Tom Brady.

00:30:22.904 --> 00:30:24.118
I was like, no idea who that was.

00:30:24.118 --> 00:30:26.081
- You are sitting next
to a Bostonian right now.

00:30:26.081 --> 00:30:27.960
My heart hurts, my heart hurts.

00:30:27.960 --> 00:30:29.441
You didn't know Tom Brady.

00:30:29.441 --> 00:30:31.782
You sat me next to someone
who doesn't know Tom Brady.

00:30:31.782 --> 00:30:34.310
- Is the twist there's a third Paul Rudd?

00:30:34.310 --> 00:30:37.474
- Well, you'll have to, this
is a no spoiler show Ryan.

00:30:37.474 --> 00:30:40.511
So I can't disclose that information.

00:30:40.511 --> 00:30:41.990
- Yeah triple Rudd.

00:30:41.990 --> 00:30:44.411
- Whew, at the thought of it.

00:30:44.411 --> 00:30:46.107
- I don't think I can handle
that I love him so much.

00:30:46.107 --> 00:30:47.831
All right guys well that is our show.

00:30:47.831 --> 00:30:50.685
I couldn't have done it
without unmasked vigilantes,

00:30:50.685 --> 00:30:52.856
Roxy, Shane and Ryan.

00:30:52.856 --> 00:30:55.684
Thank you guys so much for
joining me on the couch today.

00:30:55.684 --> 00:30:58.732
And for more on Watchmen head
on over to RottenTomatoes.com.

00:30:58.732 --> 00:31:00.900
Also please comment below and
let us know what you think

00:31:00.900 --> 00:31:01.852
about the series so far.

00:31:01.852 --> 00:31:03.298
We love reading your theories.

00:31:03.298 --> 00:31:05.117
Also don't forget to hit
that subscribe button

00:31:05.117 --> 00:31:06.362
and notification bell,

00:31:06.362 --> 00:31:08.435
so you don't miss any future episodes.

00:31:08.435 --> 00:31:10.889
Join us next time as we talk
about at 'The Mandalorian'

00:31:10.889 --> 00:31:13.089
on Disney+, so stoked.

00:31:13.089 --> 00:31:15.090
I'm Naz Perez and I'll
see you guys next time.

00:31:15.090 --> 00:31:17.673
(upbeat music)

